## 1. What are Java Annotations?

Annotations are **metadata** that you attach to Java code (classes, methods, fields, parameters, etc.).  
They don’t change the code’s logic directly, but they give information that:

* the compiler can use (e.g. to generate warnings or errors),
* build tools or IDEs can use (e.g. for code generation),
* runtime frameworks can use (e.g. dependency‑injection, persistence mapping, etc.).

Java ships with a handful of **built‑in** annotations (`@Override`, `@Deprecated`, `@SuppressWarnings`, …) and lets you define **your own**.

---

## 2. The Anatomy of an Annotation

An annotation is simply an interface annotated with a set of **meta‑annotations**:

| Meta‑annotation | Meaning |
|-----------------|---------|
| `@Retention`    | When the annotation is stored: `SOURCE`, `CLASS`, `RUNTIME`. |
| `@Target`       | Which program elements the annotation can be applied to (`TYPE`, `METHOD`, `FIELD`, …). |
| `@Documented`   | Whether the annotation should appear in Javadoc. |
| `@Inherited`    | Whether a subclass inherits the annotation from its superclass. |

```java
import java.lang.annotation.*;

@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)   // keep it at runtime
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)             // can be put on a class/interface
@Documented
public @interface Info {
    String author() default "Unknown";
    String date() default "";
}
```

* **Retention**: `RUNTIME` lets you read the annotation via reflection later.
* **Target**: `TYPE` means you can write `@Info` on a class or interface.

---

## 3. A Complete, “Hello‑World” Example

### 3.1  Define the Annotation

```java
// File: Info.java
import java.lang.annotation.*;

@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
@Documented
public @interface Info {
    String author() default "Unknown";
    String date() default "";
}
```

### 3.2  Use the Annotation

```java
// File: MyService.java
@Info(author = "Alice", date = "2024‑10‑09")
public class MyService {

    public void doSomething() {
        System.out.println("Doing something important...");
    }
}
```

### 3.3  Read the Annotation at Runtime

```java
// File: Main.java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Class<MyService> clazz = MyService.class;

        // Check whether the annotation is present
        if (clazz.isAnnotationPresent(Info.class)) {
            // Retrieve the annotation instance
            Info info = clazz.getAnnotation(Info.class);
            System.out.println("Class: " + clazz.getSimpleName());
            System.out.println("Author: " + info.author());
            System.out.println("Date  : " + info.date());
        } else {
            System.out.println("No @Info annotation present.");
        }
    }
}
```

#### Compile & Run

```bash
$ javac Info.java MyService.java Main.java
$ java Main
Class: MyService
Author: Alice
Date  : 2024-10-09
```

---

## 4. Common Built‑In Annotations (Quick Reference)

| Annotation | Typical Use |
|------------|-------------|
| `@Override` | Indicates a method is overriding a superclass method; compiler checks correctness. |
| `@Deprecated` | Marks a type/member as outdated; compiler issues a warning. |
| `@SuppressWarnings` | Tells compiler to ignore specified warnings in a scope. |
| `@FunctionalInterface` | Ensures the interface has exactly one abstract method (for lambda compatibility). |
| `@SafeVarargs` | Suppresses warnings for unsafe varargs usage in final or static methods. |

---

## 5. Why Use Annotations?

| Layer | Example | Annotation in Use |
|-------|---------|--------------------|
| **Compile‑time checks** | Enforce null‑ness, method signatures | `@NotNull`, `@Contract` |
| **Code generation** | Generate getters/setters, builders | Lombok (`@Getter`, `@Setter`, `@Builder`) |
| **Framework wiring** | Map entities to DB tables | JPA (`@Entity`, `@Table`, `@Column`) |
| **Dependency injection** | Register beans | Spring (`@Component`, `@Autowired`) |
| **Configuration** | External properties mapping | Micronaut (`@ConfigurationProperties`) |

Annotations let you keep the **logic** of your code separate from **cross‑cutting concerns** (logging, transactions, validation, etc.).

