## 7. A Note on Annotation Processing (Optional)

Java provides the **Annotation Processing Tool (APT)**. With a class that implements `javax.annotation.processing.Processor`, you can generate new source files, compile‑time checks, or other artifacts when the compiler sees your annotations. The framework libraries (e.g., Lombok, Spring Annotation Processor) use this mechanism.

For a **tiny** example:

```java
@SupportedAnnotationTypes("Info")
@SupportedSourceVersion(SourceVersion.RELEASE_17)
public class InfoProcessor extends AbstractProcessor {
    @Override
    public boolean process(Set<? extends TypeElement> annotations,
                           RoundEnvironment roundEnv) {
        for (Element e : roundEnv.getElementsAnnotatedWith(Info.class)) {
            // Do something, e.g., write a log file
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

This is usually outside the scope of a simple example but shows the power behind annotations.

---

## 8. Summary

* **Annotations** are metadata that can be read at compile‑time, run‑time, or both.
* They are defined as interfaces, annotated with meta‑annotations (`@Retention`, `@Target`, …).
* Java supplies many built‑in annotations; you can create your own for any purpose.
* A simple custom annotation (`@Info`) shows how to declare, apply, and read it at runtime.

With this foundation, you can start exploring the many powerful annotation‑driven frameworks that Java offers today. Happy annotating!
